entry containment-building
term: Containment Building
related_terms: nuclear-reactor
related_terms: defense-in-depth
related_terms: meltdown
related_terms: shielding
related_terms: reactor-core
quick_facts: Final barrier between the reactor and the public
quick_facts: Reinforced concrete ~1 m thick with a steel liner
quick_facts: Designed against quakes, impacts, and internal pressure
quick_facts: Held firm at Three Mile Island (1979)
quick_facts: One layer of the defense-in-depth strategy

sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"Safety Standards","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/safety-standards"}

definition:
    The massive reinforced-concrete dome or box — typically about a meter thick with a steel liner — that encloses a reactor's primary system. It is the final barrier in the defense-in-depth strategy: built to hold steam pressure from a loss-of-coolant accident, withstand earthquakes and external impacts, and keep radioactive material sealed inside even during severe core damage. Containment did its job at Three Mile Island (1979); at Fukushima (2011) it largely contained the meltdowns despite hydrogen explosions damaging the buildings.
